{
  "teamId": 141,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Toronto Blue Jays were never pretending to compete for a playoff spot — they were building toward something. With Vladimir Guerrero Jr. making his long-awaited debut and a wave of young talent beginning to surface, the season was always going to be measured in development milestones rather than wins and losses. A 67-95 record told the story of a roster in transition, but the individual moments scattered across the summer hinted at the future that was being assembled.\n\nThe early months offered brief optimism before reality set in. Rowdy Tellez announced himself with a three-run homer on Opening Day, and Brandon Drury delivered a pair of clutch blasts in late April that suggested the lineup might have some pop. But May proved disastrous — a 7-21 record that cratered any early goodwill — and the bullpen, anchored by shaky performances from Joe Biagini, Tim Mayza, and Ken Giles, consistently surrendered leads at the worst moments. The team was outscored by over a hundred runs on the season, and those cracks showed up in the highest-leverage situations all year long.\n\nThe summer brought reasons for genuine excitement. Vladimir Guerrero Jr. and Cavan Biggio began to flash their considerable promise, and the late-July arrival of Bo Bichette injected the franchise with electric energy. A stretch in late July featuring big moments from Drury, Teoscar Hernández, and Justin Smoak gave the team some life, and Guerrero's RBI triple off Aaron Judge in early August felt like a statement play from a generational talent still finding his footing.\n\nBy September, the losses were mounting but the future was arriving in real time. Randal Grichuk's grand slam provided one of the season's most dramatic single swings, and Anthony Alford's first career home run capped a season full of first steps for a roster being rebuilt from the ground up. The 2019 Blue Jays lost a lot of games, but they also introduced the core that would soon make Toronto relevant again — and that was always the real point.",
  "chapters": [
